1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,480
Rederive it.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,880
Don't take it on trust.

3
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:06,620
A paper reports an effect.

4
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:11,100
The statistics say it is unlikely to be chance, and the journal published it.

5
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:16,580
You can trust the paper, or you can run the study again and see whether the effect
comes back.

6
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:18,840
Trust is cheap and fast.

7
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:23,340
Running it again is slow, and it is the only one of the two that can say no.

8
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:30,760
In 2015, the Open Science Collaboration repeated one hundred psychology studies from
three journals.

9
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:34,720
Ninety-seven of the hundred originals had reported a positive result.

10
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:39,020
Of those ninety-seven, thirty-five came back significant in the repeat.

11
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:43,600
On average, the repeated effects were about half the size of the originals.

12
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,420
That does not show the other studies were wrong.

13
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:49,040
A single repeat can miss a real effect.

14
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:52,300
It shows how much a published result can rest on one run.

15
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:58,160
Re-running an analysis is cheaper than re-running a study, if the data and code
exist.

16
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:03,040
Victoria Stodden and colleagues took two hundred and four papers from Science,
published after the journal required authors to share data and code on request, and

17
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:07,320
asked for them.

18
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:13,760
They got the materials for forty-four percent of the papers, and estimated that
about twenty-six percent would reproduce.

19
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:18,760
A rule that relies on the author answering an email checks only the authors who
answer.

20
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:25,160
Web certificates are public records, so anyone can check them without asking the
issuer.

21
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:31,760
Deepak Kumar and colleagues wrote two hundred and twenty checks and ran them over
two hundred and forty million certificates.

22
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:36,180
In 2012, more than twelve percent of certificates had errors.

23
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:39,960
By 2017 the share was two hundredths of one percent.

24
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:43,380
The paper does not show the checks caused that fall.

25
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:47,980
It shows a public record can be re-checked by a stranger, at the scale of the whole
web.

26
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:52,100
A result you can re-derive does not need your trust.

27
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:55,740
Ask for the data, the code and a check you can run yourself.

28
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:58,660
The voice you heard is a synthesized version of mine.

29
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:01,840
Every number on screen links to its source under the video.
